**Answer: Yes, $f$ is continuous (in fact, $f$ has a $C^1$ representative).**

**Proof.** Let $f \in W^{1,p}(U)$ and suppose each weak partial derivative $\partial_i f$ agrees a.e. with a continuous function $g_i \in C(U;\mathbb{R})$. We show $f$ admits a $C^1$ representative on $U$; in particular it is continuous.

Let $\eta \in C_c^\infty(B(0,1))$ be a standard mollifier and set $f_\varepsilon := \eta_\varepsilon * f$, defined on $U_\varepsilon := \{x \in U : \operatorname{dist}(x,\partial U) > \varepsilon\}$.

*Step 1. Uniform convergence of the gradients.* For every $i$ and every compact $K \Subset U$,
$$
\partial_i f_\varepsilon = \eta_\varepsilon * \partial_i f = \eta_\varepsilon * g_i \xrightarrow[\varepsilon\to 0]{} g_i \quad\text{uniformly on } K,
$$
because $\partial_i f = g_i$ a.e. and a continuous function convolved with an approximate identity converges to it uniformly on compact sets (using uniform continuity of $g_i$ on a neighborhood of $K$).

*Step 2. Convergence of $f_\varepsilon$ up to constants on a ball.* Fix a ball $B \Subset U$ and a point $x_0 \in B$. Since $B$ is convex, for any $x \in B$ the segment $[x_0,x]\subset B$, and
$$
f_\varepsilon(x) - f_\varepsilon(x_0) = \int_0^1 \nabla f_\varepsilon\big(x_0 + t(x-x_0)\big)\cdot (x-x_0)\,dt.
$$
By Step 1, $\nabla f_\varepsilon \to g := (g_1,\dots,g_n)$ uniformly on $B$, so
$$
f_\varepsilon(x) - f_\varepsilon(x_0) \xrightarrow{\text{unif.}} \phi(x) := \int_0^1 g\big(x_0+t(x-x_0)\big)\cdot(x-x_0)\,dt,
$$
where $\phi \in C^1(B)$ with $\nabla \phi = g$ on $B$ and $\phi(x_0)=0$.

*Step 3. The constants converge.* Since $f \in L^p(U)$, mollification gives $f_\varepsilon \to f$ in $L^p_{\mathrm{loc}}(U)$, hence in $L^p(B)$; in particular $\{f_\varepsilon\}$ is Cauchy in $L^p(B)$. Write
$$
f_\varepsilon - f_\delta = \big(f_\varepsilon(x_0)-f_\delta(x_0)\big) + \big(\phi_\varepsilon - \phi_\delta\big),
\qquad \phi_\varepsilon(x) := f_\varepsilon(x)-f_\varepsilon(x_0).
$$
Then $\|\phi_\varepsilon-\phi_\delta\|_{L^p(B)} \le |B|^{1/p}\|\phi_\varepsilon-\phi_\delta\|_{L^\infty(B)} \to 0$, and $\|f_\varepsilon-f_\delta\|_{L^p(B)}\to 0$. Hence
$$
|f_\varepsilon(x_0)-f_\delta(x_0)|\,|B|^{1/p} \le \|f_\varepsilon-f_\delta\|_{L^p(B)} + \|\phi_\varepsilon-\phi_\delta\|_{L^p(B)} \to 0,
$$
so $\{f_\varepsilon(x_0)\}$ is Cauchy and converges to some constant $c$.

*Step 4. Identification of the limit.* Consequently $f_\varepsilon = f_\varepsilon(x_0) + \phi_\varepsilon \to c + \phi$ uniformly on $B$, while also $f_\varepsilon \to f$ in $L^p(B)$. Therefore
$$
f = c + \phi \quad\text{a.e. on } B,
$$
and $c+\phi \in C^1(B)$ with $\nabla(c+\phi) = g$. Thus $f$ coincides a.e. on $B$ with a $C^1$ function.

*Step 5. Global representative.* Cover $U$ by balls $B \Subset U$. On each ball we obtain a $C^1$ representative $\tilde f_B$ of $f$. On overlaps $B\cap B'$, both $\tilde f_B$ and $\tilde f_{B'}$ equal $f$ a.e., hence agree everywhere on $B\cap B'$ by continuity. Piecing them together yields a single $\tilde f \in C^1(U)$ with $\tilde f = f$ a.e. and $\partial_i \tilde f = g_i$ everywhere.

In particular $\tilde f \in C(U)$, so $f$ is continuous (it has a continuous representative). $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
